
  [1mThe fork:[0m where does [38;5;153muserId[39m come from? The three answers lead to different interfaces, and the wrong guess is work
  you'd throw away.

  - [1mCaller-supplied identity[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m, with the handler passing a value it gets from
    somewhere you have in mind (a tenant ID, an account ID from a URL or config). Cheap to add, but I'd be inventing the
    source, and if I guess wrong the signature and every call site change again.
  - [1mServer-assigned, so not a parameter at all[0m — the real identifier comes back from the auth response. Then the change
    is to the [3mreturn[0m value ([38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m) plus wherever you want it recorded, not to the parameter list.
    This is the usual shape for "track who logged in," and it's the one that most often gets mis-specified as a
    parameter.
  - [1mA new field the user enters[0m — add a [38;5;153muserId[39m input to the form and thread it through. Changes the UI and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m
    too; a visible product decision, not just plumbing.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second — server-assigned — because a login function can't be told the identity of the person
  logging in before it authenticates them; that value is an output of login, not an input. But you know the system this
  stub stands in for and I don't, so I'd rather ask than guess.

  One more thing worth naming: "track who logged in" is a separate question from the parameter. Today tracking is a bare
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m. If you want actual tracking (persisted, sent to an analytics or audit endpoint), that's a new subsystem
  this repo doesn't have, and it would push this past bounded into its own design conversation. If you just mean
  "include the ID in the existing log line," that stays bounded and I can do it in the same change.
